    "explanation": "Both sides agree on the arithmetic, and the platform's own record settles it. The driver arrived at the pickup at 09:47:05 (EV-TRIP-05054, corroborated by the GPS trace EV-GPS-05054, whose nearest fix is 2.73 m from the pickup, inside the 50 m STANDARD arrival radius) and cancelled at 09:51:25 — 260 seconds later. Under POL-NS-21 the free wait runs for 300 seconds (5 minutes) from the driver's arrival, i.e. to 09:52:05, and the record's own flag confirms cancelled_after_free_wait = false. POL-NS-22 authorises the 5.00 SGD no-show fee only 'when the driver cancels after the free wait'. That condition was not met, so the 5.00 SGD charged at 09:51:29 (EV-FARE-05054; refund_issued_sgd 0.00, so the rider still carries the whole of it) was not authorised and must be returned. The rider is owed the entire contested sum: FULL_REFUND of 5.00 SGD, with driver_compensation_sgd null. The driver's counter-statement that he 'waited more than 5 minutes' is not borne out on the wait, and the driver's own submission concedes the 260-second figure; the 40-second shortfall is what voids the fee, and nothing further is taken from the driver beyond losing the fee.\n\nOn the driver's other points: his contact obligation under POL-NS-23 was in fact met — two in-app messages (09:47:30 and 09:49:50) and one in-app call (09:50:30, unanswered after 22 s), all before the 09:51:25 cancellation — so the fee is not voided on that ground. But POL-NS-23 governs contact only; it does not shorten, waive or excuse the POL-NS-21 free wait, and it cannot convert a cancellation made inside the free wait into a lawful no-show. His submission that the rider's refund 'rests on no clause' is rejected: the refund flows from POL-NS-22's own precondition failing together with POL-NS-21's free wait.\n\nThe rider's argument that the wait should be measured from the first stationary fix (09:46:50) is not the policy's start — the free wait starts from trip_record.arrived_at, settled as 09:47:05 — but it makes no difference: even measured from 09:46:50 the wait would not expire until 09:51:50, still after the 09:51:25 cancellation. The rider's statement that she was 'already walking down' is carried by no artifact (the trace carries no rider track and the chat log carries no rider message), and the driver's point that it rests on no evidence is noted, but the outcome does not turn on it.\n\nNo referral arises. POL-NS-24's 5.00 SGD goodwill credit requires both a failure to arrive by the deadline and a failure to attempt contact; the driver arrived and attempted contact, so it is not engaged. The fee charged (5.00 SGD) matches the fee POL-NS-22 sets, so no referral on the amount arises. The rider's no-show count is below POL-NS-25's threshold of 3, so no repeat-no-show flag arises. POL-GEN-26 escalation is not open: every fact these rules need is carried on COMPLETE artifacts at weight 1.0, and the only absent artifact (EV-ADVIS-05054-01, a traffic advisory, weight 0.0) is required by no no-show clause. The dispute value is 5.00 SGD, below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD band with no fraud flag, so this ruling is final at any confidence."
